From e9b6d54bdf33aa92241222356053db12aced129f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Opsero Electronic Design Inc." <support@opsero.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 20:27:15 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] net: axienet: HSE monitor - interval-latched status, robust
 no-signal rule, cumulative counters

Third round of fixes for the HSE link monitor, from the second hardware
retest of the 2x QSFP28 FMC 40G design (ZCU106):

1. Interval sampling. RX status (and on l_ethernet block lock) are
   latched-low, clear-on-read. The monitor used to flush them and judge
   an immediate second read, i.e. a few microseconds. Now every sample
   reads them twice: the first read says whether the RX was good during
   the WHOLE interval since the previous sample, the second read gives the
   current state. The interval is only trusted when no reset of ours lies
   inside it: after open and after every recovery reset it is discarded,
   and HSE_LINK_ARM_MS (100 ms) after each reset the registers are flushed
   so that the next interval starts after the reset has completed.

2. No-signal rule. A dark receiver showed block lock on some lanes
   (0x5/0x6/0x7) in the flush-and-read sample, so it was taken as "signal
   present"; three failed RX-only resets then led to a reset-all (28 in a
   60 s dark period), each of which pushed the lit direction through a
   re-alignment. Now the signal counts as usable only if ALL four lanes
   were block-locked AND the RX was aligned continuously over the interval.
   Otherwise: GT RX datapath reset only, 500 ms for the first three, then
   every 1 s, every 2 s after ten, never a reset-all. Reset-all remains
   for GTWIZ reset-done not set (PLL/refclk) and for a usable signal with
   an RX that still does not work after three RX-only resets.

3. Healed outages visible. A partner re-alignment of ~0.75 s that fell
   between two 1 s healthy samples went unnoticed. A healthy sample whose
   interval read shows a loss of status/alignment/block lock now counts
   hse_rx_unaligned_events and logs a rate-limited info line; the carrier
   is not changed.

4. MAC statistics. The monitor's TICK windows (every sample) are summed
   into 64-bit ethtool counters hse_mac_rx_total, _good, _bad_fcs,
   _stomped_fcs, _fragment, _truncated, _bad_code (l_ethernet only), so the
   error counts no longer depend on the design's 14-bit wrapping FIFO
   error-drop field. Tools that write TICK_REG themselves take those
   windows away from these counters.

5. rx_dma_pkt_drop is now cumulative across down/up: the MCDMA S2MM
   packet drop count is added to a 64-bit software counter just before
   every DMA reset.
